# CHANGELOG lines that claim a speed and that no benchmark case measures, each with the reason (scripts/check-bench-cases.sh).
# Format: a phrase of the line (it must stay in the Unreleased section), a tab, the reason. A line that a case measures needs no entry here (its row of bench/cases.tsv names it);
# an entry whose phrase matches no claim, or that has no reason, fails the check. Short on purpose: a speed that can be measured gets a case, not a line here.
`point_columns` (`sift_col` reloption, default none	an access-method lookup (sift.enable_col_scan on, a few rows returned), timed per query by bench/col_scan.py (kind point, next to the btree, classes and set_columns kinds); the cases of bench/cases time aggregate plans (SiftAgg), which a point lookup does not use
an index scan of `sift_col` that selects a few rows of a unit	an access-method lookup, timed per query by bench/col_scan.py (kinds point, classes, set); the cases of bench/cases time aggregate plans, which a point lookup does not use
the `sift.col_*_ops` classes of a `sift_col` index cover boolean	an access-method scan, timed by bench/col_scan.py (kind classes) next to the btree
a `sift_col` index built with the operator classes `sift.col_int2_ops`	a bitmap index scan, timed by bench/col_scan.py (kind classes) next to the btree
the aggregate scan counts the tuples of a chunk once	measured in user instructions per execution (2 to 7% of one query), below what the wall-clock rounds of a case resolve; the counts are in the line
the aggregate scan finds the NULL tuples of an integer	measured in user instructions and branch mispredictions per execution (+1.1% instructions), not in time
is read by walking the elements the key marks	measured in user instructions (-3.7%), below what the wall-clock rounds of a case resolve
translated the bound of each partition again	measured in instructions of the start of a scan of 4000 partitions (48 to 30 million), not in time
the table `VACUUM` honours the cost delay	a throttle: the VACUUM is slower on purpose, as an autovacuum worker is; no speed is claimed for it
the extension SQL goes from 3.2 to 3.3	an upgrade notice; the numbers in the line are version numbers
`scripts/plangate.sh`, a plan-choice gate	a test gate that times its own shapes (win, tie or lose at 1 ms) for a human to read; not a benchmark of one optimization
`test/t/pg_upgrade.sh` runs `pg_upgrade --check`	a test script; the numbers are PostgreSQL versions
records why a per-unit key presence summary	records a design that was not built; its 7.8x and 2.0x are from a prototype
leave the visibility map of the table empty	the same measurement as cluster_visibility_map (a scan priced for a heap check after CLUSTER, 320 ms against 82 ms forced)
the UNION ALL arms of a two-level or fused scan go from 4 to 16	a limit on the width of a plan shape (slug shape_width), not a speed
the group table of a `Custom Scan (SiftAgg)` is bounded by `hash_mem`	bounds peak memory (VmHWM) and spills by design, so it is slower where it spills; the case would measure memory, which the runner does not
the reloption `return_columns = 's, u'` of a `sift_col` index makes the named text	an access-method index-only scan, off by default; its own numbers are a loss in memory (0.055 against 0.155 ms), timed by bench/col_scan.py like the other access-method scans
is stored in blocks of 64 strings that a reader decompresses one at a time	the first statement of a new backend (a cold dictionary), timed per backend (median of 3 to 5 new backends) by hand and by the focused tests; the cases of bench/cases time repeated plans, in which the parsed dictionary is cached, so the speed they would show is the warm one that the line says does not change
`sift.col_auto_merge` (off, 3.2) also vacuums the table	timed by the update, delete and VACUUM section of the README (bench/steps/store_section.py, lane sift_maint) with the setting on, against the same lane with it off; not an aggregate plan, which the cases of bench/cases time
